==English==

===Etymology===
{{lbor|en|la|anadiplōsis}}, itself a borrowing from {{der|en|grc|ἀναδίπλωσις}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|anadiploses}}
{{examples|examples=* "{{nowrap|Suffering produces endurance, and}} {{nowrap|endurance produces character,}} and character produces hope."<br>(Romans 5:3-4)
* Fear leads to anger. Anger leads to hate. Hate leads to suffering.<br>(''Star Wars Episode I: The Phantom Menace'')}}

# {{lb|en|rhetoric}} A [[figure of speech]] in which a word or phrase used at the end of a [[clause]] or [[expression]] is repeated near the beginning of the next clause or expression.
#* {{RQ:Langley Rhetoric|80|'''''Anadiplosis''''' ends the former line<br>With what the next does for its first design.}}

====Usage notes====
Frequently combined with (but distinct from) [[climax]], so that each step of the anadiplosis typically increases in magnitude or rhetorical force, with the effect of making the last term more powerful by comparison.

